::STAT_U02_L01_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L01_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
What is the mean of 6, 8, 10, and 12?
{
~A. 8#This is one data value, not the mean.
~B. 10#This is one data value, not the mean.
=C. 9#The sum is 36, and 36 divided by 4 is 9.
~D. 36#This is the sum before dividing.
}

::STAT_U02_L01_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L01_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
What is the median of 11, 13, 14, 18, 29?
{
~A. 13#This is below the middle value.
~B. 18#This is above the middle value.
=C. 14#The ordered middle value is 14.
~D. 29#This is the maximum, not the median.
}

::STAT_U02_L01_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L01_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Which measure of center is most affected by a very large outlier?
{
~A. Median#Median is resistant to outliers.
=B. Mean#The mean uses all values, so an extreme value can pull it.
~C. Mode#Mode depends on frequency, not distance from other values.
~D. Interquartile range#IQR measures spread, not center.
}

::STAT_U02_L01_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L01_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
A data set is 5, 5, 7, 9, 11. What is the mode?
{
~A. 7#7 occurs once.
=B. 5#5 occurs most often.
~C. 9#9 occurs once.
~D. 11#11 occurs once.
}

::STAT_U02_L01_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L01_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
A salary data set has one extremely high salary. Which measure usually gives a better typical salary?
{
~A. Mean#The high salary can pull the mean upward.
=B. Median#The median is less affected by one extreme high value.
~C. Range#Range measures spread, not center.
~D. Maximum#The maximum is the outlier, not a typical value.
}

